      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To evaluate the prevalence of symptomatic uterine fibroid in France, related symptoms and its impact on quality of life."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Online prospective survey from December 2012 to February 2013 using two questionnaires on 2498 women aged between 30 and 55 years old and representatives of the feminine population."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "On the studied population, 220 women presented a symptomatic uterine fibroid representing a prevalence of 8.8%. 163 women (73.7%) presented bleedings associated or not with pain, and 58 (26.3%) suffered from pain only. For 77 (34.9%) women, the symptoms preceded the diagnostic and the mean delay between first symptoms and diagnostic was 2.22 years (ET : 3.56). At the time of the survey, 144 (65%) women with symptomatic uterine fibroid were followed by a physician (a gynecologist in 91.6% (n=132)), and 110 (49.7%) evaluated their pain as severe or extremely severe and 178 (80.6%) were bothered by their symptoms in their everyday life."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "This analysis shows a strong prevalence for symptomatic uterine fibroid with a major impact on the quality of life."
          }
        ]
      },
